  "body": {
    "id": "prop:binary-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "If the menu is specialized to \\(M=2\\), then \\(d=1\\), \\(L(r)=L_{\\sqrt{\\Sigma(r)}}(\\mathcal H)\\), and the exact scale identity is \\(L_{\\sigma}(\\mathcal H)=\\sigma L_1(\\mathcal H/\\sigma)\\). If \\(0<\\sigma_1\\le\\sigma_2\\), then \\(N(h,\\sigma_1^2)\\) Blackwell dominates \\(N(h,\\sigma_2^2)\\) by addition of independent Gaussian noise, so \\(L_{\\sigma_1}(\\mathcal H)\\le L_{\\sigma_2}(\\mathcal H)\\). Therefore every design minimizing the single contrast variance is regret-optimal; no converse or strict monotonicity is asserted without an additional condition on \\(\\mathcal H\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(M\\)",
      "\\(d\\)",
      "\\(L(r)\\)",
      "\\(r\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal H\\)",
      "\\(\\Sigma(r)\\)",
      "\\(\\sigma\\)",
      "\\(L_{\\sigma}(\\mathcal H)\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:gaussian-game",
      "thm:gaussian-value-characterization"
    ],
    "justification": "The binary reduction is the required sanity check and isolates why three overlapping policies are the first nontrivial case.",
    "gap": "Binary Gaussian treatment-choice and Neyman allocation results are known; this proposition verifies exact compatibility rather than claiming novelty.",
    "consumer": "Readers can diagnose whether the new regret criterion changes design only because the action menu creates genuinely multivariate tie geometry.",
    "proof_tex": "When \\(M=2\\), the definition \\(d=M-1\\) gives \\(d=1\\), and the game in \\(\\mathrm{def:gaussian\\text{-}game}\\) observes \\(N(h,\\Sigma(r))\\).  By the definition of the binary benchmark,\n\\[\n L(r)=L_{\\sqrt{\\Sigma(r)}}(\\mathcal H).                              \\tag{1}\n\\]\nIf \\(Z=h+\\sigma\\varepsilon\\), put \\(g=h/\\sigma\\).  Then \\(Z/\\sigma\\sim N(g,1)\\), and positive homogeneity of binary regret gives\n\\[\n \\ell_{\\mathrm{reg}}(h,j)=\\sigma\\ell_{\\mathrm{reg}}(g,j).\n\\]\nThe maps \\(h\\leftrightarrow g\\) and \\(Z\\leftrightarrow Z/\\sigma\\) are bijective because \\(\\sigma>0\\).  Taking the same infimum over rules and supremum over the reparameterized state set therefore proves\n\\[\n L_\\sigma(\\mathcal H)=\\sigma L_1(\\mathcal H/\\sigma).                \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nNow let \\(0<\\sigma_1\\leq\\sigma_2\\).  Given \\(Z_1\\sim N(h,\\sigma_1^2)\\), generate independently \\(E\\sim N(0,\\sigma_2^2-\\sigma_1^2)\\).  Then \\(Z_1+E\\sim N(h,\\sigma_2^2)\\).  Consequently the smaller-noise experiment can reproduce, by this Markov kernel, the risk vector of every rule in the larger-noise experiment.  Taking minimax values yields\n\\[\n L_{\\sigma_1}(\\mathcal H)\\leq L_{\\sigma_2}(\\mathcal H).            \\tag{3}\n\\]\nThus a design minimizing the sole contrast variance also minimizes the binary regret value in (1).  The simulation argument proves only the weak inequality (3), so it supplies neither a converse nor strict monotonicity without an additional condition on \\(\\mathcal H\\).",
